not the `cobblestone-u62rel` worktree convention; no local worktree exists yet.

## What upstream did with ours

The release notes say they took issue 153 with PR 154, PRs 155 and 156, and
issue 157. `u62-candidate` is U61 + PRs 154-156 (U61.log), and all three PR
heads sit directly on U61, so measuring each PR head against U62 file by file
covers the 8 files the candidate touches:

     6 byte-identical   empty-list-as-{integer,text}.{codex,failing} (PR 154),
                        act-unused-bind.{codex,expected} (PR 156)
     0 prose only
     1 code as sent     ZigEmitter.codex: both PRs present, file otherwise moved
     1 dropped          compiler-backlog.md: PR 154's COMPILER-85 row

No prose was edited this time, unlike U61. The emitter diffs of PRs 155 and 156
carry no comments (one table row; two changed lines in each of the two `act`
emitters), so "as sent" there is code only. The one prose change we sent, PR
154's backlog row, was dropped whole rather than reworded.

- **PR 155 (`bit-not`)**: our table row is byte-identical at ZigEmitter:1103.
  The patch no longer reverse-applies only because its context moved:
  `list-length` no longer follows `bit-shr` in the table.
- **PR 156 (unused `act` bind)**: both hunks reverse-apply cleanly on U62's
  ZigEmitter, offset -44 lines. Landed verbatim.
- **PR 154's backlog row**: not carried. Our row filed the empty-list hole as
  COMPILER-85; upstream fixed it outright (main 26013) and gave COMPILER-85 to
  the issue's SECOND shape instead: desugared tuples, revised records and
  derived `show` names losing their instantiated IR types (main 26027). Neither
  number names the empty-list defect in U62's backlog. The two refusal tests
  are theirs now, unchanged.
- **Issue 157**: all three items acted on, none measured here yet.
  1. The literal table rebuilt at every reference is COMPILER-86: the
     reproducer went from 528 bytes per read to 0 (main 26048); the compiler
     shares 32 of its 41 tables, the Zig plug shares constant tables (main
     26148), record fields and a left `&` operand are covered (main 26370).
     Three tables remain unshared, named in
     `docs/Designs/Active/Compiler/ConstantSharing.md`. COMPILER-86 is still
     open in the backlog for the non-literal case.
  2. Zig plug allocations no longer bypass std's `0xAA` poisoning (main 26044).
  3. The plug refuses `ReleaseFast` and `ReleaseSmall` and benches at
     `ReleaseSafe` (main 26086). Ours that this breaks:
     `outbound/evidence-raytracer-nearest-first/{bench2,verify-bench}.sh`
     both run ReleaseFast. They are sent evidence, so a record, not a fix.

ZigEmitter.codex as a whole moved +157 / -60 over U61; PRs 155 and 156 are a
few lines of that. The rest (issue 157 items 1-3 and whatever else) is
unread.

The notes credit "the fixes and the initial measurements" to us, "developed
with Claude", and claim the regression fixtures and the release proof as
theirs.

Ours to follow, from the notes alone and not yet run:

- Frontend changes the Rust arm will have to meet. Checked method templates
  and per-demand dictionary slots (COMPILER-90). A constrained function over
  a class with ONE instance is now specialized to it, so a call at a type
  with no instance is a type error (main 27104). `[] == []` needed its own
  release-time fix (main 27130). And the empty literal is list-typed.

## cobblestone-qemu: GREEN 5/5 in 8m58s, after four reds that were all ours

    ~/runs/20260925T110102Z-u62             checkers b0f9312
    ring plug 23s · fib 7s · zigemit 37s · codexir 3m29s · x86emit 4m13s
    verify   zig PASS · qemu PASS (IR identical to the native tool) · x86 PASS · boot PASS (6765)

Run in a cloud container (tcg, no KVM, 4 cores), not the droplet, so the
times are not comparable with U61's 12m43s.

Every red was the harness failing to keep up with how U62 changed upstream's
BUILD, not a defect in U62:

1. **Ring plug: `Undefined name: shared-const-names`.** The Zig plug calls
   IR/ConstShare (COMPILER-86); upstream's `plugs/zig/build.ps1` bundles it
   via a new `-CompilerChapters`. `bundle_ringplug.ps1` now reads that from
   the checkout (`9ab698a`).
2. **fib transpile REFUSED on a `@compileError`.** U62's plug emits a
   top-level comptime refusal of ReleaseFast/ReleaseSmall into every program:
   issue 157 item 3, as landed (main 26086). It is fixed prelude text, so it
   went into `prelude-comptime-guards.txt`, which had been empty since U54.
3. **codexir: nine CDX3002s.** The checker and X86_64Chapter call into the
   two new chapters without citing them. `bundle_ir_to_x86.ps1` kept its own
   list of 64 compiler chapters; it now reads `build/compiler-order.txt`
   (upstream's build refuses a chapter with no row there) and names only the
   ten it leaves out, each with its reason. Same lines on U62 as the hand list
   plus the two new chapters; only the order moved, to upstream's.
4. **codexir: IRTextMeta has no `method-templates`.** U62 moved IR emission
   into `prepare-method-ir` (lift, `eq-attach-helpers`, prune,
   `method-materialize`). Our harness was a copy of the driver, and reading
   the diff showed the copy had ALREADY drifted: it never attached the
   equality helpers the driver has attached since COMPILER-44. So a program
   using generic equality got different IR from native codexir than from the
   seed. fib uses none, so the qemu check's IR diff could not see it. The
   harness now CALLS `compile-frontend-ir` -> `prepare-method-ir` ->
   `emit-ir-chapter` with `-WithDriver` (`b0f9312`): U61's "next" item 7,
   which had proposed exactly this. Its first run found one
   more stale list: `-WithDriver` named six foreword chapters that
   `assemble_unit.ps1` has added itself since U60, so every type in them came
   out twice (CDX3001 x15). Removed.

Re-run with codexir's harness carrying the hosted deck prologue that
codex-zig-transpiler's harnesses put in front of the same driver calls
(`823c5b1`; `b0f9312` had none and had only run fib):

    ~/runs/20260925T112535Z-u62             checkers 823c5b1
    GREEN 5/5 in 8m47s; codexir 3m26s
    eq-generic-recursive through native codexir | zigemit: __eq_ helpers on
    the wire, MATCHES all 9 expected lines

The principle, now in README: when an Update makes us edit our own chapter
lists or harnesses, read how upstream changed its side first, and derive from
their files.

Ours to follow:

- The equality-helper drift, dated: `eq-attach-helpers` is on the driver's IR
  path from U56 (`6cd2ca1`) on and absent through U55; no cobblestone-qemu
  commit before `b0f9312` mentions it. So cobblestone-qemu's native codexir
  lacked the driver's `__eq_*` helpers for U56-U61. codex-zig-transpiler's
  harnesses had the same gap: measured below.
- `emit_harness.py`'s `frontend_source` still stands in for the driver in the
  x86emit harness. Same question, not yet asked.
- A stray `main` branch on cobblestone-qemu (my mistake, at `e2321e1`, equal
  to `master`) is Steve's to delete; deleting it was refused from here.

## codex-zig-transpiler: the fixed point HOLDS at U62

    fixed point   byte-identical, 3,156,931 bytes of zig (U61: 3,007,652)
    arith.codex   MATCHES all 9 expected lines
    309s cold     stage 4 guest 105s (peak 723 MB) · stage 5 guest 103s (1430 MB) · NATIVE transpile 66s
    oracles       codexir + codexcheck rebuilt (113s, no guest), both smokes pass

`f0acd43` (source) and `711a0bf` (`generated/` repinned). The same three
build fixes as cobblestone-qemu (ring plug `-CompilerChapters`, the guard,
`compiler-order.txt` for the one chapter list, leaving out only BootPaint and
EntryPoint), and both harnesses now take the driver's IR front door,
`compile-frontend-ir` -> `prepare-method-ir` -> `emit-ir-chapter`.

For CodexZigHarness that is more than tidiness. Pass 1 of the fixed point is
the seed in IR mode, which IS that front door; pass 2 used to reach the same
IR through `compile-frontend-cdx`'s `cdx-ir` and a copied prune. At U62 the
front door also runs `method-materialize`, so a neighbouring path would have
been one Update from breaking the fixed point for a reason that was never the
emitter's.

**The equality-helper gap, measured** on upstream's
`codex/test/eq-generic-recursive` (resolved with `resolve_unit.ps1`):

    U61 codexzig   1 __eq_ def (the desugarer's __eq_Named); every generic ==
                   dangles, and the plug emits @compileError("zig plug: no
                   emitter for __eq_Pair@Text"): the program does not build
    U62 codexzig   4 defs (__eq_Box_82Integer, __eq_Box_82Text,
                   __eq_Pair_82Text, __eq_Named); MATCHES all 9 expected lines

So the gap FAILED LOUD in codexzig, a refusal at zig build rather than a wrong
answer, which is why nothing banked a wrong result from it. The IR oracle is the
softer case: `~/codexir` at U56-U61 emitted IR with the dangling reference
and no error, so an IR diff against the seed would differ there. Whether any
curated or Rust-graded unit uses generic equality over a parameterised type is
the question for those arms at U62.

Correction to this log's earlier line: U61 did not skip this repo (U61.log
has "the fixed point HOLDS at U61"). I had read only U61.log's head and tail.

